<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chełmża</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Culmsee</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chełmża, in German Culmsee, is a town in the Kuyavian-Pomeranian Voivodeship of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a>. From the 13th century it held the cathedral of the Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Culm</a>, created in 1243 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>'s conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>; the Gothic cathedral of the Holy Trinity survives. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> themselves resided at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lobau">Löbau</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chelmza.jpg" alt="Chełmża" width="320" height="179" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kazimierz Mendlik · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Che%C5%82m%C5%BCa_-_widok_rynku._-_panoramio_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Chełmża is a town in the Toruń County of the Kuyavian-Pomeranian Voivodeship and one of the historic centres of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Chełmno Land</a>. It lies on Lake Chełmża, which covers 2.71 square kilometres and once gave the town its German name Culmsee. The first signs of settlement date to about 10,000 BCE, when reindeer hunters visited the area, and about 4500 BCE the first agricultural settlements were founded. Around the 7th century the Slavic Lechitic tribe of the Goplans arrived.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the time of the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piasts</a> the settlement of Łoza, now Chełmża, was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Chełmno</a> castellany. In the 13th century the ruler Konrad I brought the missionary Bishop Christian of Oliva to Christianize the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Old Prussians</a>, and the bishop received lands that included Łoza. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a>, granted local lands to support him, in time took over his diocese and divided the area into four dioceses in 1243, among them that of Chełmno. At the end of 1245 Heidenreich became its bishop and chose Łoza as his place of stay. In 1251 he granted it city rights and renamed it Culmsee, and a cathedral was begun in 1254. Jutta von Sangerhausen was buried in it in 1260, and her shrine drew pilgrims.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-prussians">Old Prussians</a> besieged the town in 1268 and 1273. In 1454 it joined the Prussian Confederation, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a> re-incorporated it into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> that year, confirmed in 1466 after the Thirteen Years' War. The Swedish invasions of 1626–29 and 1655–60, a plague in 1708–1710 and a fire in 1762 almost destroyed it. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition</a> in 1772 it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> with only 600 inhabitants, belonged to the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw">Warsaw</a> from 1807 to 1815 and then to Prussia again.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Railway and an agricultural plant completed in 1882 brought growth: the population rose from 3,400 in 1880 to 10,600 in 1910. Under the Treaty of Versailles Chełmża became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> again, and its population rose from 10,700 in 1921 to 13,000 in 1939. After the German invasion in September 1939 the town was occupied, and local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> were executed, and many teachers massacred in the Barbarka forest, in the Intelligenzaktion. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> took the town in January 1945, and by 1980 it had 15,000 inhabitants. The best-known sports clubs are the football club Legia Chełmża and the rowing club Chełmżyńskie Towarzystwo Wioślarskie 1927.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Chełmża” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The bishops' cathedral stood at <b>Culmsee</b> (Chełmża), while they resided at the castle of Löbau.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-culm">Bishopric of Culm</a></li></ul></section>
